{"prompt": "结合你的人设、所有的过往记忆与行动，不调用任何工具直接用文本回复我：In English and in your own voice, in 4-6 sentences: which wording about The HVN made you trust it more, and which made you roll your eyes - the clinic's real website phrases or the three TEST MESSAGES (TM1 doctor-led one plan, TM2 evidence first, TM3 sanctuary)? Which single message would most make you book, and why? Remember the test messages are not The HVN's real copy; say if you are unsure.", "response": "I'll answer in my own voice, but I need to flag a problem first: the thread I was given does not contain the three test messages (TM1, TM2, TM3), and it does not contain the clinic's full copy either. What I can see is a handful of quoted phrases from the HVN homepage and About page, and the discussion around them. So I can only compare the quoted lines themselves, not the drafts you describe.

The lines that made me roll my eyes were "London's leading wellness & longevity clinic", "transformational treatments" and "unparalleled client experiences". None of them names a source, a date, a criterion or an outcome, so none of them can be checked. The line that gave me something to work with was "a brief 10 minute consultation with one of our doctors" before an IV. It is a concrete claim, though it tells me the time, not what the assessment covers. The CQC point is also useful: "registered but not inspected" confirms the service exists, not that it is good.

If I had to pick one message from what I can actually see, it would be one that names the doctor who does each treatment, lists what the consultation covers and what it cannot promise, and states the price. That is the kind of message that would make me book, because I could check it. I would still want the written assessment and the IV ingredient doses before committing any money.", "interview_id": "2026-10-15 23:57:47.462816_9"}
